**My answer to the prompt:** Add centering rules to the parent. For example, give the parent a class and style it with Flexbox:

```html
<div class="container">
  <img src="img/world.png" alt="World map">
</div>
```

```css
.container {
  width: 100vh;
  height: 100vh;
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;
  align-items: center;
}
```

This centers the child horizontally and vertically without changing the child’s CSS. If you want the parent to fill the viewport, `width: 100vw` is usually more appropriate than `100vh`.

**Evaluation:** Assistant A gives the correct, concise Flexbox solution and respects the request to use only the parent’s CSS. Its example uses the broad selector `div`, which could affect other divs on the page; a class selector would be safer.

Assistant B also describes valid Grid and Flexbox approaches, but its code examples contain malformed HTML (missing `<` characters and invalid closing tags), including in the complete-page example. It is also much more verbose than needed. The valid core advice is useful, but the broken examples make it less reliable than A’s answer.

Both answers could mention that `100vh` sets the width based on viewport height, not viewport width. **Assistant A is better overall.**

[[A>B]]

A suitable parent-only solution is:

```css
.parent {
  width: 100vh;
  height: 100vh;
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;
  align-items: center;
}
```

```html
<div class="parent">
  <img src="img/world.png" alt="World map">
</div>
```

This centers the child element(s) horizontally and vertically within the parent. If you intend the parent to fill the viewport’s width, use `100vw` for its width rather than `100vh`.

Assistant B is better. It gives valid, directly applicable CSS and correctly explains that flexbox centers the child without changing the child’s CSS. Assistant A describes valid centering approaches, but its code examples are malformed: they omit `<` characters and use invalid closing tags, so they cannot be copied and used as written. It is also much more verbose than needed. Neither answer notes that `100vh` sets the width relative to viewport height; that may be intentional, but `100vw` is more typical for a full-viewport-width parent.

My final verdict is: [[B>A]]

